hoot and most of  the weapons can be dual wielded which  works really well because you don't  to lose any accuracy by not aiming down  the sights this lens combat a sense of  finesse  despite its fast pace the enemy AI isn't  all that complex but it still works in  the context of this kind of shooter  instead larger enemies like Nazi robots  add variety to combat through their  increased threat and the fact that  different tactics are required to take  them down and all the while the combat  feedback is dialed to eleven levels flow  back and forth really nicely through  tight corridors and wide open arenas  though enemy numbers in those levels  never reach the access of say do more  Serious Sam like hordes there isn't a  variety in the combat spaces and the  enemy combinations within that the new  orders levels feel well paced and combat  feels tense without being unmanageable  or overwhelming some rudimentary yet  functional stealth mechanics allow the  new order to craft entire levels where  blasts kovitch is armed with nothing but  a knife these quiet tense sections add  variety to the game's pacing the  presence of nazi commanders who can call  in reinforcements if they detect you  create a hierarchy of high-value targets  in a single room it's rewarding to feel  like you're stealthily stalking them  taking them out silently and then you're  free to pull out the big guns and clear  an area in the most efficient manner  possible with these mechanics along with  some interesting mission locations and  stellar environmental design the new  order offers a wide variety of combat  experiences blasts kovitch can unlock  both stealth and combat perks but the  tasks required to do so like stealth  killing a certain number of Nazis are  mostly actions that you perform  naturally over time the perks themselves  provide only subtle effects like  slightly extra ammo so you'd be forgiven  for forgetting that their system exists  at all  weapon upgrades which are found  throughout the game's levels offer more  tangible benefits like additional  tactical avenues assault rifles can be  upgraded to fire underslung rockets and  shotguns can be made to fire shells that  bounce off walls effectively turning  them into  real tournaments flak cannon you also  need to make use of a laser cutter but  it's mostly relegated to cutting Blas  kovitch sized holes in the only pieces  of metal grating that are blocking your  forward progress in the first place both  this laser cutter and the perk system  feel like missed opportunities at worst  even aside from them the new orders  combat intensity and variety has granted  the Wolfenstein series a breath of fresh  air whilst still managing to hit the  nostalgic highs that I expect from the  series the game has even injected some  substance into the primal pleasure of  shooting Nazis by way of an interesting  tone that addresses the changing roles  of first-person shooter protagonists  just kidding  through this the game is both a  celebration of and what feels like a  fitting send-off for the Wolfenstein  series as a whole the new order could be  the last row of William BJ Blazkowicz an  outing which for all its success and  bombast  is far from mindless  Oh  okay
 

